  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The volume, measured in cubic centimeters, of water in a cup of coffee\n- The average length, measured in millimeters, of hairs on a horse\n- The number of hairs on a cat",
  "worked_solution": "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,\u2026,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
